#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Renders a Claude Code session transcript as a simple prompt / answer page.

Reads the session's JSONL under ~/.claude/projects/<project-slug>/ and writes a
self-contained HTML file: a numbered list of your prompts (sidebar, with a search box) and,
for each, the assistant's final answer rendered from markdown with a "copy answer" button;
intermediate assistant text (progress notes between tool calls, replies to background
notifications) is folded under each turn. Tool calls, tool results and thinking are left
out. Stdlib only.

Usage (from the project directory, e.g. with Claude Code's `!` prefix, whose output is added to the
conversation's context; with respondToBashCommands false in settings.json Claude does not reply to it):
    claude-conversation                 # latest session of this directory -> HTML, prints its file:// URL
    claude-conversation --open          # ... and open it in the browser
    claude-conversation --open --auto-refresh 30   # page reloads itself every 30 s (see below)
    claude-conversation --session <id-prefix> | --jsonl <file> | --all   # other sessions
    claude-conversation --watch 20 --open   # regenerate every 20 s until Ctrl+C (with auto-refresh)
    claude-conversation --serve         # ... and print an http:// URL instead (see below)
    claude-conversation --stop-server
Output: ~/.claude/conversations/<project-slug>/<session-id>.html (override with --out). Every run also
rewrites ~/.claude/conversations/index.html, which links every session page under that directory (all
projects, grouped by project). Only one file:// URL is printed: the page's, or the index's if several
sessions were rendered. With -v, one line per session is printed instead (HTML path, the session's
name as `/resume` lists it, and its prompt count), then the index URL. --open uses xdg-open on each
generated page, so it does nothing useful over SSH: open the printed URL instead. Each page starts with a
title line, "claude conversations" (linking to the index from session pages), followed by when it was
generated (so it advances under --watch) and, with --serve, the server's address, host name and pid.

--auto-refresh is for pages that something other than --watch regenerates: you re-running the command,
or e.g. a Claude Code Stop hook. Once a page has it, runs regenerating that page must pass it again
(they exit with an error otherwise, rather than silently stopping open tabs from reloading), or pass
--auto-refresh 0 to turn it off.

--serve starts a background HTTP server for ~/.claude/conversations on all interfaces (port 10041 by
default; --port), unless it is already running, and prints http:// URLs instead of file:// ones.
Their host is the address this machine was reached at over SSH (from $SSH_CONNECTION, so the link
works from the SSH client's browser), else localhost. The server has no access control: anyone who
can reach the port can read every rendered session. It runs until --stop-server. When a --watch run in
a terminal ends (Ctrl+C), it asks whether to stop the server: anything but "n", including another
Ctrl+C, stops it.
"""
